tricacies of kubernetes deployments  and all of the  kubernetes concepts that go along with  it  so you can say that cloudrun for anthos  gives you the best of both worlds  gives you the best of serverless you can  run any standard docker container  uh it has request based auto scaling  it's very fast and scales down to zero  includes incremental rollouts and  rollbacks so you can do things like blue  green deployments and roll back from  and it also has a whole suite of  monitoring and logging tools that are  built in for the developer out of the  box  and at the same time the operations team  gets all the benefits of  kubernetes environment the flexibility  and control that comes with gke  integrated policy support the ability to  run your compute completely within a vpc  custom node types and customization of  the underlying hardware  and the extensive toolset that the  kubernetes ecosystem provides  and i'd be remiss if i didn't mention  that cloud run for anthos is based on  the k-native open source project  this is an open source project that was  started by google with contributions  from over 80 companies  and really what this guarantees is that  the workloads that you write and  architect for cloud run for anthos  are portable between k-native  environments in whichever cloud platform  you ultimately end up choosing  so now let's talk about actually  building an architecting web application  so let's imagine for a moment  you're a developer in an e-commerce  company  gke in kubernetes is the ops platform  that your company has chosen and  standardized  and you're given a task with delivering  a line of business web application for  tracking your warehouse inventory on a  pretty tight timeline  so it's monday morning you sit down with  your cup of coffee you sharpen your  pencil  and you start sketching out the  architecture that you're going to use  and this is sort of what's in your head  to begin with you know that it's going  to run in gcp  you know that there's going to be a web  application of some kind and you know  that  it ultimately needs to run on both  desktop laptop and mobile devices  but then you really start to break it  down you know you're going to need a  front end  you're going to you're going to need a  back end and a database to store your  state  you start breaking it down some more and  and some more requirements really start  to come into view  since your company is standardized on  gke you're going to use cloud run for  amthos for your back end services  for all the serverless benefits in the  kubernetes platform we talked about  earlier  and also you know you're going to need  to access and modify  data in a database in a secure fashion  so in addition to using firestore as a  database  you want to be able to use your  kubernetes service identities for this  authentication  and you'd be remiss if you hadn't  thought a little bit about how load  balancing  and your urls were going to work and you  realized because you want to serve your  front end from a status storage bucket  but have your back end services running  in cloud run for anthos you need the app  to be on a single endpoint and subdomain  for coors reasons and you need  path-based routing between the front-end  served from gcs and your multiple  back-end services running on cloud run  for anthos  and also your enterprise has a hardcore  requirement that everything needs to be  https out of the box  as it should be and that your gke  cluster can't be connected to the  internet  and finally as the last requirements  start to come into view  you realize that you need to make sure  this web application is accessible only  by employees they absolutely have to  authenticate  and they need to use your company's  federated identity provider  also there's different role-based  features for workers  and managers of the warehouse so you  need to have role-based access and an  authorization platform for each call  that goes through  you're going to use istio for that  so after a couple of weeks of hard work  of architecting and writing the  application  you're ready to hand it off and start  managing inventory in your warehouse  using an application you designed to run  on cloud run for anthos  but you sort of thought to yourself it  should be great if there was a blueprint  for all of this  and it turns out that there is the cloud  run for anthos reference web application  you can see the link it's hosted up  there on github today and it's an  opinionated set of best practices aimed  at  demonstrating a reference architecture  for building an enterprise-grade web  application on gcp  using cloudrent for anthos so now i'm  going to kick it over to my friend adam  and he's going to take you through each  of the  components of the architecture that we  talked about a little bit earlier  and go into a deep dive about how we  implement those and how you can  implement them in your environment  adam thank you justin  as justin mentioned i'm going to walk  you through a little bit of a deeper  look  at some of the decisions that we've made  in implementing our  reference web application  so we'll start with a quick overview of  the reference architecture  that we've used the goal of our  reference application is to simulate  a line of business web application  that's built around the cloud run for  anthos platform  the subject matter of our application in  this case is inventory management  but this is mostly arbitrary the goal of  this application is to provide  a reference for implementing common web  application patterns  using cloudrin for anthos in combination  with other gcp services  in this case we've implemented the  application using angular  and go but the infrastructure patterns  that we demonstrate here are not  specific to these languages  so as a quick overview of the  architecture  it's comprised of a front-end ui and  multiple back-end  stateless services it's exposed through  a google cloud load balancer which  provides a consolidated endpoint to our  end user  the front-end ui is statically served  from a gcs bucket  and our back-end services are hosted  using cloudrun for ampos  end user authentication and  authorization of our backend api  requests are handled by istio  in combination with the google identity  platform and our application state  is stored in a firestore database  let's now take a look in a little bit  more detail about each of the three  major infrastructure patterns that we've  used in a reference application  first we'll talk about the database that  stores state for  our application of course our backend  services  need to be able to access and modify the  data  in this database in a secure fashion  ideally we should be able to  access this database using the service  identities for our backend services  and not have to manage explicit secrets  or other authentication tokens we use  firestore in this case which is  compatible with gcp  im authentication but this discussion is  really relevant  to any database or service which  provides authentication using iam  our backend services because they're  running on cloud run for anthous are  bound to kubernetes service accounts or  ksas  we use a feature of gke called workload  identities  to bind these kubernetes service  accounts to google service accounts or  gsas  in iam once this binding is in place  we can apply our access permissions for  our database to the google service  accounts  and through workload identity these  permissions apply transitively to our  kubernetes service accounts  once we've enabled workload identities  on our cloud road  for anthos cluster binding service  accounts  is a two-step process that requires  action in both  gke and iam in gke  each kubernetes service account is  annotated with the gcp service account  annotation  which specifies the google service  account that we are binding the  kubernetes  service account to  in iem a policy binding is created