ckend bucket  associated with the gcs bucket that  contains our static application assets  it also includes path specific routes to  multiple backend services  which are each mapped to individual  cloud run for antho services running on  our cluster  by default a custom host header is  required to ensure that requests  received by the cluster ingress  are sent along to the correct service on  our cluster  in this case we're using gclb to inject  these host header values  and to make sure that our requests are  sent to the appropriate service on the  cluster  in the case of the reference application  we've configured our load balancer to  terminate ssl  we've used a google managed ssl  certificate for you  for this case  because our end user traffic always  starts at the gclb endpoint  our cluster ingress doesn't need to be  exposed directly to the internet  traffic to the ingress of the cluster  can be restricted to only be allowed  from  trusted sources such as gclb's  well-known ingress ranges  this can be achieved through either  using a private cluster  or as in the case with our reference  application using firewalls to restrict  where that traffic can originate from  when you provision our reference web  application with the automation we've  included in github  we'll actually automatically apply these  ingress traffic rules  to restrict access to the cluster  there's a lot of individual resources to  provision  and manage with a load balancer like  this  the config connector or kcc provides the  ability to manage all of these resources  using krm and they do this by defining  kubernetes crds for each of these gcp  resources  in the case of our reference application  we install kcc  on the same cluster used to host our  backend services  infrastructure for our application can  then be provisioned and managed  by creating kcc resources on the same  cluster  you can reference and reuse any of the  infrastructure configuration that we  talk about in this presentation  by referencing the krm in our github  repository  you can connect cloudrun for anthos to a  gclb load balancer  using a standalone network endpoint  group or an neg  cloudrun for anthos uses istio to manage  its request ingress to the cluster even  when you don't explicitly install istio  we package a minimal version of it with  cloudrun for anthos when you're  installed on the cluster  all requests that are bound for user  services on your cloud run for anthos  cluster  first arrive at what's called the istio  ingress gateway service  on the cluster and are then routed from  there to the appropriate user service  to connect to gclb we are going to  create a network endpoint group  associated with the ingress gateway on  our cluster  to do this we add a cloud.google.com  neg annotation to the istio ingress  gateway service resource and this is  just a standard  kubernetes service resource in response  gke will add the neg status annotation  automatically to that service resource  and this annotation will include the id  and the zone of the associated network  endpoint group  we'll now create a backend service to  associate with this network endpoint  group  we want to bind our gclv backend service  to a specific end user service on the  cluster  so we configure the backend service with  a  custom request header for the host  header field  of the request cloudrun for anthos  uses this host header value to determine  where to route the requests once they  arrive at the istio ingress gateway  service  all of the requests as a result  forwarded to this backend service  will arrive at our desired  user service on the cluster  the network endpoint group is then added  as a back end  to this backend service using its id and  zone that we received from the gke  annotation in this case the backend  service is associated with  foo.example.com and we can now include  this backend service in the url map of  our load balancer so that we can route  requests to it  in the examples on the slide we've  showed gcloud commands  to provision these resources but it's  also possible to  provision these resources using config  connector you can see an example of this  if you look at the krm resources in our  github repository  finally we're going to cover how we  handle end user authentication  and authorization for the reference  application  as a refresher we're building an  application here for internal use by  employees so it's important that we  authenticate  each end user that arrives at our  application  we would like to use a federated  identity provider  and we'd also like to implement email  and password authentication  as well we're going to need role-based  authorization to allow us to limit  access to certain features  on the application based on a custom  user role  assigned to each user in this case users  can be either workers  or managers  let's take a look at how we've solved  authorization and authentication on  our reference application so we've  chosen to use google as our federated  identity provider this allows end users  to sign into the application with their  google account  we've also implemented email and  password authentication  however this is mostly to easily enable  certain testing scenarios  covering how this works and to end  when a user authenticates with our  application  they receive a json web token  and this json web token is used by the  front end  uh to make api calls to our backend  services  when a request arrives at the istio  ingress gateway  istio is configured to validate these  tokens with the gcp identity platform  and also enforce role based role-based  authorization  for various methods and apis on our  backend services  here you can see an example of the istio  authentication policy created to  validate these json web tokens  against the google identity platform in  this case it applies to all requests  with paths that begin with the slash  api prefix  we also configure authorization in this  case with istio resources and you can  see an example of that here  in this case this policy authorizes  access to the post method of the  inventory transaction api  for end users with the role type worker  all of the authentication and  authorization resources used in a  reference application can be found in  our github repository  i'm now going to hand it over to justin  for a demo of how  to provision the reference web  application in your gcp project  now i'm going to give you a quick demo  of how easy it is to get the sample up  running and deployed into your gcp  project directly from github as you can  see here i am  on the github repository for the sample  application  and here is the readme and this readme  actually has a step-by-step walkthrough  and includes scripts that will get the  sample built and deployed into your  cluster which i'm going to show you a  little bit of right here  you can see there's a big blue button  that says open in google cloud shell  what this actually does is it clones  this repository into  in your gcp cloud shell vm and from here  you can edit the code and then follow  along with the readme to deploy it  right into your gcp environment um so  i've already got this set up ahead of  time but what i am going to do  uh is go ahead and run the included  script to kick off cloud build  and get the application deployed into my  environment  i'm gonna kick off a make  and as you can see it's getting started  there and i'm going to hop over here to  cloud build and you'll see that that has  triggered a cloud build  it should be running now um you can see  that that build is running  uh from what i kicked off in cloud shell  there and it's it's fetching the source  from the  uh latest commit  now given this is going to take a little  bit of time to set up  go through all these steps and set up  the infrastructure i'm going to skip  ahead in time  to a build that already completed and  show you that i've got the back end  services for the application running in  cloud run for anthos  and you can see there's a user service  and a back end service  for different components of the api that  the application is calling  um and uh over the last hour you can see  some  metrics hasn't been a whole lot of  utilization because i just spun this up  but you can see the request count the  number of containers instances for the  back end etc  and then finally i can actually just  show you the application  running live here i can click  the g button to log in  now i've logged in it's going to load  the inventory of items  there we go perfect  so to sum everything up cloudrun for  anthos gives you the best of serverless  development patterns  in a kubernetes environment by  integrating  with the suite of other gcp services  it's possible to implement an  enterprise-ready web application  using cloud run for anthos and the rest  of the anthos and gke ecosystem  and finally everything we just showed  you is open source and available on  github  for you to try and deploy into your own  gcp environment  so on behalf of adam and everyone here  at google cloud  we want to thank you for watching our  presentation and hope you have a  wonderful rest of your day

